Trace this dial, where a helper raises whatever object it is handed:
class Dial { int v; Dial(int v){ this.v = v; } int get(){ return v; } static void raise(Dial target, int by){ target.v = target.v + by; } } Dial d = new Dial(10); Dial alias = d; Dial.raise(d, 5); System.out.print(d.get() + "/" + alias.get());
What is printed?

A crate class promises a caller an object of its own. A client builds one box, asks it for a copy, writes 9 into the copy, and finds the original reporting 9 as well:
class Box { int v; Box(int v){ this.v = v; } int get(){ return v; } Box copyOf(){ return this; } } Box a = new Box(4); Box b = a.copyOf(); b.v = 9; System.out.print(a.get());
The program prints 9. What is the fault?

A vault class refuses a short code while one is being built, yet a client ends up reporting 7:
class Vault { private int code; Vault(int code){ if (code < 1000) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("short"); } this.code = code; } void importCode(int code){ this.code = code; } int getCode(){ return code; } } Vault v = new Vault(1234); v.importCode(7); System.out.print(v.getCode());
The program prints 7. What is the fault?

A node class adds itself to a shared list before it tests its argument:
class Node { static java.util.ArrayList<Node> LIVE = new java.util.ArrayList<Node>(); int v; Node(int v){ this.v = v; LIVE.add(this); if (v < 0) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative"); } } } Node.LIVE.clear(); try { new Node(5); new Node(-1); } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { } System.out.print(Node.LIVE.size());
The program prints 2. What is the fault?

A key class offers an identity test, and a client uses it on two separately built keys:
class Key { String code; Key(String code){ this.code = code; } boolean sameAs(Key other){ return this == other; } String get(){ return code; } } Key held = new Key("A1"); Key fresh = new Key("A1"); System.out.print(held.sameAs(fresh));
The program prints false even though both keys read A1. What is the fault?

A pair class promises that a plus b is always 100, yet a client trips the check on a brand new object:
class Pair { int a; int b; Pair(int a){ this.a = a; } void check(){ if (a + b != 100) { throw new IllegalStateException("sum"); } } int getB(){ return b; } } Pair p = new Pair(40); try { p.check(); System.out.print("valid"); } catch (IllegalStateException e) { System.out.print("sum is " + (40 + p.getB())); }
The program prints sum is 40. What is the fault?

A range class promises that lo is never above hi, yet a client ends up with a range that runs backwards:
class Span { int lo; int hi; void setLo(int lo){ this.lo = lo; if (hi < lo) { hi = lo; } } void setHi(int hi){ this.hi = hi; } int show(){ return lo * 100 + hi; } } Span s = new Span(); s.setLo(2); s.setHi(9); s.setHi(1); System.out.print(s.show());
The program prints 201, a range whose low end sits above its high end. What is the fault?

A cell holds a tag, and a client keeps a reference to the tag it read before asking for a different one:
class Tag { String code; Tag(String code){ this.code = code; } String get(){ return code; } } class Cell { Tag label; Cell(Tag label){ this.label = label; } Tag get(){ return label; } void relabel(Tag next){ label = next; } } Cell c = new Cell(new Tag("a")); Tag held = c.get(); c.relabel(new Tag("b")); System.out.print(held.get() + "/" + c.get().get());
The program prints a/b. What is the fault?
